---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FeatureRow from '../components/sections/FeatureRow.astro';
import VideoEmbed from '../components/sections/VideoEmbed.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/sections/CtaBand.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How it works — DocketWorks"
  description="A board for every job, timesheets and POs wired into the jobs, quoting that knows what materials cost, and the reports a whiteboard never gave you."
>
  <section class="bg-primary-50 py-20 md:py-24">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-[var(--max-w-site)] px-6">
      <h1 class="max-w-3xl text-3xl font-bold leading-tight tracking-tight text-primary-900 md:text-4xl">
        How it works
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-primary-700">
        Underneath, it’s a board for every job, with timesheets and purchase
        orders wired straight into the jobs — plus quoting, reports and scheduling.
        Here’s the shape of it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FeatureRow
    heading="What it looks like"
    image="/placeholder-screenshot-board.svg"
    imageAlt="The DocketWorks job board — columns of job cards, with tabs for drawings and more"
  >
    <p>
      A board, much like Trello’s — every job is a card you can see at a glance,
      moving through the stages of your shop. Open a card and there are tabs for
      the things that job needs: drawings, costs, the timesheet entries against it,
      the lot.
    </p>
  </FeatureRow>

  <FeatureRow
    heading="Keeping every job moving"
    image="/placeholder-screenshot-board.svg"
    imageAlt="A job card on the board, showing its stage, due date, and who it’s assigned to"
    reverse
    bg="light"
  >
    <p>
      This is the strongest part of the system. Every job has a stage and a due
      date; you can see the whole shop’s worth of work in one view, spot what’s
      slipping, and reassign a job to someone else in a couple of clicks — and see
      straight away what that does to your delivery dates.
    </p>
    <p>
      When someone’s off sick or on leave, the work doesn’t live in their head —
      it’s on the board.
    </p>
  </FeatureRow>

  <FeatureRow
    heading="Timesheets and purchase orders, wired into the jobs"
    image="/placeholder-screenshot-timesheet.svg"
    imageAlt="A timesheet entry in DocketWorks, with the time going against a job"
  >
    <p>
      When a staff member fills in their timesheet, it does two things at once: it
      gets them paid, and it puts that time against a job. No double entry, no
      “I’ll reconcile it later”.
    </p>
    <p>
      Same with a purchase order: raising it orders the material <em>and</em> puts
      the cost against the job it’s for. So at any moment you can see what a job
      has actually cost you in hours and materials — not what you guessed it would.
    </p>
  </FeatureRow>

  <FeatureRow
    heading="Quoting"
    image="/placeholder-screenshot-quote.svg"
    imageAlt="Building a quote in DocketWorks, with material prices pulled in automatically"
    reverse
    bg="light"
  >
    <p>
      It knows what materials cost — it keeps your suppliers’ price lists up to
      date for you, so a quote starts from real numbers. There’s a chatbot to help
      you draft one, you can refine a quote and see its old versions, and there’s a
      counter for how many days a quote’s been sitting waiting on the customer.
    </p>
    <p>
      Solid, and it does the job — it’s the part we’re still sharpening.
    </p>
  </FeatureRow>

  <FeatureRow
    heading="Reports and scheduling"
    image="/placeholder-screenshot-reports.svg"
    imageAlt="DocketWorks reports — billable-hours trends, quote acceptance, pipeline"
  >
    <p>
      The picture a whiteboard never gave you: how your billable hours are trending
      day to day and week to week, how many quotes you’re winning, what share of
      the team’s time actually gets billed, and whether the pipeline’s full enough
      to keep everyone busy.
    </p>
  </FeatureRow>

  <VideoEmbed
    poster="/placeholder-demo-video-poster.svg"
    posterAlt="DocketWorks demo video"
    caption="Watch the ~3-minute tour."
    bg="light"
  />

  <CtaBand headline="Have a poke around it yourself." subcopy="A real, working system — no call, no pitch." />
</BaseLayout>
